<!--Post: 12590--><!--Time: 1112505585-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12590"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxfire<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxfire<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 9:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->According to &#34;Nightmare in Wichita&#34;, Vicki Wegerle was the only victim they know about at least who managed to scratch Dennis Rader somewhere as &#34;tissue&#34; from under her nails was scraped and preserved until later when they ran a DNA test on it.   <BR> <BR>Unfortunately, the test was not run until Dennis Rader had sent the copy of her driver&#39;s license to the Wichita Eagle on March 17, 2004!   <BR> <BR>My main question at this point is how on earth did he explain his scratched face, hand, or arm to the wife and to people on the job????!!!! <BR> <BR>Of course, his wife may have gotten confused to a point at least since the police decided early on that Mr. Wegerle had probably killed her. <BR> <BR>According to the book, it was Captain Al Stewart&#39;s retirment party the same day as she was murdered, and the police discused the case during the party and decided that her husband had probably killed her. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12590-->
<!--Post: 12591--><!--Time: 1112508713-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12591"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 10:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->They did the same thing with the Fager case. When he was aquitted after, what the jury said was a wandering prosecution, during which the jury was left with the impression that they were twisting the evidence to fit their case,he was aquitted. <BR> <BR>Wpd was sure it was him. Did they look for more convincing evidence &#40;the case still has BTK written all over it [with possibly a Butterworth assist]&#41;?...Dropped the whole thing. <BR> <BR>Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap, Clap.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12591-->
<!--Post: 12592--><!--Time: 1112509576-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12592"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxfire<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxfire<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 10:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Right.   <BR> <BR>There was semen in the hot tub, but apparently testing proved was not Butterworth&#39;s.  Yet, I haven&#39;t read anywhere that it matched Dennis Rader either.  Of course, they may STILL not have tested it even if they kept the sample. <BR> <BR>There seems to be NO reason for Butterworth to have fled as far as Florida if he was not involved in some manner.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12592-->
<!--Post: 12601--><!--Time: 1112544024-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12601"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Itty<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Itty<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 9:00 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->That is preposterous. If they suspected Vicki&#39;s hubby, why in hell not do the dna testing to clear <BR>him, let him get on with his life.  <BR> <BR>I guess they didnt think they could afford the $50 or so that is costs, well guess what, now the taxpayers get to pay for a very expensive defense. <BR> <BR>I have read the Fa&#42;er dna was too degraded after being in the hot tub, high temps, for 12 hours<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12601-->
<!--Post: 12606--><!--Time: 1112548262-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12606"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 10:11 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Blinders and egos. Once the police zero in on someone, they won&#39;t let go. Why do the logical and clear the only suspect that they have? That would mean that they have another killer on their hands. And most family members are suspects in homicides. They figure that family members know each other so well that they would have a motive. <BR> <BR>Scratches: <BR>I fell into some bushes, looked under my truck and did not clear my head before coming out from under it, etc. <BR> <BR>His wife may be a &#39;yes wife&#39; that takes everything at the husband&#39;s word and questions nothing. The fact that she went into seclusion instead of speaking out probably hints of her overall demeaner. I do not know what I would do in that situation, so I cannot criticize her. <BR> <BR>If they still have DNA from the Fager homicides, new technology may be able to get some hint as to a profile, at least in eliminating a suspect. And that would be worth something. I am going to check with a biochemist I know about blood types degrading to Type O after 24 hours. I will report back. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12606-->
<!--Post: 12623--><!--Time: 1112604015-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12623"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 04, 2005 - 1:40 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Teardrop: <BR> <BR>It&#39;s worse after a trial where the defendant is acquitted.  In the unlikely event that the cops pull their head out of the sand and catch the real killer, his lawyer uses the prosecution&#39;s witnesses and evidence from the first trial to show reasonable doubt. Its the SODDI defense &#40;some other dude did it&#41; <BR> <BR>For Example: <BR> <BR>Think of 95% certain as &#34;beyond a reasonable doubt&#34;.   <BR> <BR>In the first trial the DA prosecutes John Doe for murder, but has only enough evidence to make Doe&#39;s jury 70% certain Doe is guilty.  The jury acquits John Doe. <BR> <BR>The cops, seeing the error of their ways, arrest Richard Roe for the same murder. <BR> <BR>At Roe&#39;s trial his lawyer uses the same evidence &#40;which made the first jury 70% certain that Doe was the killer&#41; and argues that Doe was the Dude who did it.  However, Roe&#39;s lawyer only needs to make Roe&#39;s jury 6% certain that Doe was the Dude to win, and his evidence does that with 64% to spare. <BR> <BR>This is the forensic equivalent of shooting fish in a barrel. <BR> <BR>Phoenix   <BR> <BR>  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12623-->
